//! run
//! link: ffi/helpers.c
//! signal: ILL
//! stdout:
//| before
// A call to a `noreturn` function is followed by `call void @llvm.trap()` and
// `unreachable` (D19.7), which is `ud2` on x86-64, so a callee that returns
// anyway dies by SIGILL with no message (D11.4). The helper is a separate
// translation unit declared `noreturn` on this side only, so nothing but the
// trap stands between the call and the next statement: this is the one
// runtime-observable consequence of D19.7. It is a live witness and not only
// a mirror of the emitted-text assertions in test/gen_call_test.c: deleting
// the `call void @llvm.trap()` in gen_expr.c and leaving the `unreachable`
// alone makes this test die by SIGSEGV instead, so it goes red.
import std.rt;

extern fn noreturn helper_returns_anyway();

fn i32 main() {
    println("before");
    // The trap ends the program without flushing the runtime's buffers, as an
    // abort does (D11.4), so what must be seen is flushed first.
    rt.fort_rt_flush_all();
    helper_returns_anyway();
}
